\title{haishoku: colour palettes from Sanzo Wada's 'A Dictionary of Color Combinations'}
\description{
Sanzo Wada published \emph{Haishoku Soukan}, six volumes of colour
combinations, in 1933-34. Seigensha's 2010 re-edition, \emph{A Dictionary of
Color Combinations} (Japanese title \emph{Haishoku Jiten}), presents 348
combinations of two, three and four colours drawn from 159 named colours,
each with a CMYK printing recipe. \emph{Haishoku} means "colour arrangement".
}
\details{
Where to start:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=haishoku_palette]{haishoku_palette()}} and \code{\link[=haishoku_show]{haishoku_show()}} to get and look at a combination.
\item \code{\link[=scale_fill_haishoku]{scale_fill_haishoku()}} and friends for 'ggplot2'.
\item \code{\link[=haishoku_combos_with]{haishoku_combos_with()}} for the book's dictionary: which combinations a colour
appears in. \code{\link[=haishoku_show_colour]{haishoku_show_colour()}} draws them.
\item \code{\link[=haishoku_find]{haishoku_find()}} to rank combinations by distinctness and colour vision
deficiency (CVD) safety; \code{\link[=haishoku_extend]{haishoku_extend()}} when you need more colours.
\item \code{\link[=haishoku_cmyk]{haishoku_cmyk()}} and \code{\link[=haishoku_export]{haishoku_export()}} for print and other tools.
}

Every \code{haishoku_} function also has a short \code{hs_} alias, e.g.
\code{hs_palette()} for \code{\link[=haishoku_palette]{haishoku_palette()}}.

Set \verb{options(haishoku.combo = <number>)} to change the default combination
used by the scales and \code{\link[=theme_haishoku]{theme_haishoku()}}.
